# Atomic Commits & PR Workflow

Developer-facing git text (commits, PR titles/bodies, changelogs) uses **clear short English
first**. Tracking IDs (task, milestone, feature, ADR, session, decision) are optional
**citations** in parentheses or body footnotes — never the only readable part of the subject.
See [developer-facing-language.mdc](developer-facing-language.mdc).

## Commits

Every commit must be **atomic** — one logical change, codebase valid after each commit.

1. **One task per commit**: Each execution-plan task gets its own commit. Do not bundle
   multiple tasks into one commit. Track which task in `workflow-state` / the plan; do not
   lead the subject with opaque codes like `T1.1` or `M1`.

2. **Commit message format**: `type: short English summary`
   - Prefer conventional types: `feat`, `fix`, `test`, `docs`, `chore`, `refactor`, `hotfix`
   - Optional cite after the English: `(F75)` / `(ADR-052)` / `(task 127.5)` when useful
   - Examples:
     - `test: add preprocessing unit coverage`
     - `fix: correct input validation on ingest options`
     - `chore: configure ruff linter`
     - `refactor: extract shared export utilities`
     - `docs: add staging runbook`
     - `hotfix: restore admin pagination (#112)`
     - `chore(skills): update connectivity gates`
     - `feat: enqueue catch-up for failed embeds (F75)`
   - Avoid: `[T1.1] test: …`, `[M1] refactor: …`, subjects that are only codes

3. **Post-commit checks**: After every commit, the codebase must:
   - Pass all existing tests
   - Pass the linter
   - Pass the typechecker
   If any check fails, fix it in the same commit (amend) — do not leave a broken commit.

4. **After push to origin**: Run GitHub verification per
   [ci-after-push.mdc](ci-after-push.mdc) — `bash scripts/ci/watch_github_ci.sh [branch]`
   until required workflows pass (`ci.yml` always; `deploy-preflight.yml` on `main`), or fix
   and push again.

5. **No WIP commits**: Every commit is complete and reviewable. If work is in progress,
   do not commit until the task is done.

6. **Commit-as-you-go**: Skills must commit before:
 - Transitioning to the next pipeline stage
 - Asking the user a blocking AskQuestion
 - Running a gate check
 - Ending a session/turn
 Never leave uncommitted work across session boundaries.

7. **No solo `workflow-state.yaml` commits** (do not sync state alone — RET-001 RA-005): Do
 not create a commit whose *only* change is `workflow-state.yaml` unless you are **closing
 a gate or stage** (or the user explicitly asked for a state sync). Bundle state with the
 real artifact/code commit. After every commit, append to `workflow-state.yaml`
 §`git_history.commits` with sha, branch, message, stage, files_changed, and timestamp via
 workflow-state-manager (see `.cursor/skills/workflow-state-reference.md` §Git history).

## Branches

Follow the branch naming from `.cursor/skills/workflow-state-reference.md` §Git history.
Prefer an English slug; optional tracking tokens are fine when they help uniqueness:

| Change type | Branch pattern | Base |
|-------------|----------------|------|
| Feature / milestone | `feat/{english-slug}` | `stage` when available; else phase branch / AskQuestion |
| Bug fix (hotfix) | `fix/{english-slug}` | Prefer `stage`; `main` only via AskQuestion |
| Docs-only change | `docs/{english-slug}` | `stage` when available |
| Skill / tooling | `chore/{english-slug}` | `stage` when available |
| Infra / deploy | `infra/{english-slug}` | `stage` when available |
| Evolve cycle | `evolve/{cycle-id}-{english-slug}` | `stage` when available |
| Phase | `phase/{n}-{english-slug}` | `stage` when available |
| Promote to prod | `stage` (or promote PR) | `main` (second PR only) |

**PR base must:** When `origin/stage` exists, open integration PRs with `--base stage`
(see `stage-before-main.mdc` / EV-036-D15). Do not PR feature work straight to `main`.

- Create the branch before starting the first task of a milestone.
- Record every branch in `workflow-state.yaml` §`git_history.branches`.
- When a branch is merged or closed, update its status (never delete the entry).
- Legacy `feat/M{N}-…` names may appear in history; do not require that form for new work.

## Pull Requests

### Minor PR (per milestone)

When all tasks in a milestone are complete:

1. Verify all milestone tasks are `completed` in the execution plan
2. Run tests, linter, typechecker — all must pass
3. Create a PR from the milestone branch to **`stage`** when `origin/stage` exists;
   otherwise to the phase branch / AskQuestion. Prefer `--base stage` (EV-036-D15).
4. PR title: short English **behavior** name — e.g. `Monorepo scaffold` or
   `Corpus automations catch-up (F75)`. Optional cite in parentheses; do not lead with
   `[M1]`, `[EV-027]`, or `W1`
5. Auto-generate PR body from the milestone's task list, spec references, and results —
   use English summaries; cite task/feature/ADR IDs where they help navigation
6. Include the PR checklist from the execution plan §Git Strategy
7. Present the PR to the user for review — do not merge without approval
8. Opening a PR does **not** end the implementation session by default; after recording it
   in the PR Plan table, continue with the next milestone or phase when `build-execution.mdc`
   allows (unless the user asked to stop at each PR)

### Major PR (per phase)

When all milestones in a phase are complete and the phase gate check passes:

1. Verify phase gate criteria are met (see Phase Gate Check in execution plan)
2. Record the gate check in the Phase Gate Log
3. Create a PR from the phase branch to **`stage`** when that branch exists; otherwise
   AskQuestion (create `stage` / waive). Do **not** default major PRs to `main` while
   `stage` is available (`stage-before-main.mdc`). Merge into `stage` when **CI** is green
   on that PR (`staging-smoke` is not required on the feature→`stage` hop).
4. Open a **promote** PR `stage` → `main` — never skip the stage hop when `stage` exists.
   Merge promote only when **CI** + **`staging-smoke`** are green (smoke runs on PRs
   targeting `main` via `deploy-staging.yml`).
5. PR title: short English phase **behavior** name — e.g. `Foundation phase` or
   `Corpus tags and browse (EV-001)`. Avoid leading with only `Phase 1:` / `EV-NNN` unless
   paired with a readable name
6. PR body includes: gate check results, milestone summaries, test results (English first;
   IDs as citations)
7. Present the PR to the user for review — do not merge without approval
8. After recording the PR in the PR Plan table, continue runnable work in the same session when
   `build-execution.mdc` allows (unless the user asked to stop at each PR)

After creating a PR, update the PR Plan table in the execution plan with the PR URL
and status (`open` / `merged` / `closed`).

## Release changelog

Commits and PRs are structured, but **no file is auto-updated** for end users. When cutting a
release or completing deploy, aggregate merged work into `docs/CHANGELOG.md` and/or the deploy
report (see `.cursor/skills/considerations.md`). Changelog bullets must be readable English;
group by theme or feature name, with optional ID cites — not by bare `[T…]` / `[M…]` prefixes.
